.TH CEVENTLOOP_MODIFY 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ceventloop_modify \- change an existing fd registration's direction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval ceventloop_modify(ceventloop loop, ceventloop_reg reg,
                        cselect_dir new_dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Changes the direction of reg in place, with no moment where its fd is
unregistered. Only fd registrations can be modified. A registration
made from a queue or a cchan selectable gives ccol_invalid_args,
because the direction of a queue selectable is part of its identity;
remove such a registration and add it again instead.

On success:
  - reg->sel.dir is updated, so a callback dispatched after this call
    returns sees the new direction in its sel parameter. The dispatch
    path copies reg->sel under its own internal lock at the moment it
    collects each dispatch and keeps no live pointer into reg->sel, so
    a callback that is already in flight keeps the direction that was
    current when it was collected and never races with this update.
  - reg moves to the other slot on the shared epoll entry of its fd.
  - The fd's combined epoll interest mask is recomputed from the live
    state of both directions, normally with a single EPOLL_CTL_MOD
    call. reg may be paused at the time of the call (see
    ceventloop_pause(3)); if it is and the fd has no other
    direction that is not paused, the mask carries no real interest.
    The pause has already put the fd outside the kernel's epoll
    interest set, the fd stays there, and no epoll_ctl call is needed
    at all. A reg that the loop stopped watching because an event
    reached it that none of its handlers can take (see
    ceventloop_add(3)) is watched again in its new direction.

When new_dir is already reg's direction nothing else changes and the
call returns ccol_success, but a reg that the loop stopped watching as
described above is watched again.

The call only works when no other registration on the same fd holds the
slot of the target direction. A typical use is a non-blocking connect,
which starts with interest in writing and switches to reading once the
connect finishes, without a second ceventloop_add() call.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop     ceventloop the registration belongs to.
reg      Registration to modify.
new_dir  cselect_read or cselect_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The direction is changed.
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CEVENTLOOP_NULL, a stale handle,
                    or a handle that has already been destroyed; or
                    reg is CEVENTLOOP_REG_NULL or fails to resolve
                    for another reason; or reg is a registration for a
                    queue or a cchan; or new_dir is neither
                    cselect_read nor cselect_write. A reg that
                    fails to resolve can be plainly invalid, or it can
                    have been removed after the caller got it, by
                    another thread or by reg's own callback removing
                    itself. Both cases are caught through reg's
                    registration table and refused cleanly, never with
                    a use-after-free.
ccol_not_permitted  A different registration on the same fd already
                    holds the target direction.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call at the same time as ceventloop_remove(), and from a
callback running on one of the reactor threads.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ceventloop_handlers handlers = { .on_writable = on_connected, .on_readable = on_data };
ceventloop_reg reg = ceventloop_add(loop, cselectable_fd(sock, cselect_write),
                                    handlers, ctx, NULL);
/* ... inside on_connected, after the non-blocking connect finishes ... */
ceventloop_modify(loop, reg, cselect_read);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ceventloop_add (3),
.BR ceventloop_remove (3),
.BR ceventloop_pause (3),
.BR ceventloop_resume (3),
.BR ceventloop_reg_generation (3)
